<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mauricio Hochschild</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1881">1881</a>–<a href="/years/1965">1965</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mauricio Hochschild was a German-born mining entrepreneur who became one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>'s three '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> barons', alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-patino">Simón Patiño</a> and the Aramayo family. His company controlled a large share of Bolivian tin and other mineral exports until the nationalisation of the great mines in 1952. In the late 1930s he helped thousands of Jewish refugees from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> to settle in Bolivia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mauricio-hochschild.jpg" alt="Mauricio Hochschild" width="320" height="250" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Irene Bran · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1026470_F82658.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hochschild was born on 17 February 1881 in Biblis into a Jewish family that had been active in mining for more than a generation. He was the eldest son of Louis Hochschild Altschul, a general trader whose cousins Berthold Hochschild and Zachary Hochschild were in the metal industry, the first as founder of the American Metal Company and the second as a partner in Metallgesellschaft. After school he studied mining and engineering at the Freiberg University of Mining and Technology.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1905 Hochschild joined the industrial conglomerate Metallgesellschaft, which made him its agent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. He then worked independently in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, spending several years in Chile with his brother Sali Hochschild, and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> until the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. In 1918 he married Käthe Rosenbaum, who died in 1924, and in 1920 he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>. Known there as Don Mauricio, he built over the next two decades a business in the mining and trade of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> ore that stretched from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> to Chile, and the Moritz Hochschild Group reached the height of its economic and political influence in the 1930s.</p><p>In 1938 he used his influence with German Busch, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>'s military president from 1937 to 1939, to have the country admit Jewish refugees from Nazi Germany, and he paid for their transport and housing. About 9,000 were admitted by one estimate; others put the number he helped escape between 9,000 and 22,000, and he has since been called the Bolivian Schindler. The Bolivian government arrested him and sentenced him to death in 1939 and again in 1944, and two weeks after his release in 1944 he was held by kidnappers for two weeks. Once freed he left Bolivia for good.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1951 the Hochschilds gave most of their fortune to the Hochschild Trust and Foundation. When the group was nationalised the following year during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivian-national-revolution">Bolivian National Revolution</a>, they were compensated with 30 per cent of its former assets, and the company grew into Hochschild Mining. In 1961 he opened the Mantos Blancos copper mine at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antofagasta">Antofagasta</a> in Chile, his most successful operation. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 12 June 1965 and was buried in Père Lachaise Cemetery.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Moritz Hochschild” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From about 1900 tin replaced silver as the main export, making the mine owners Simón Patiño, <b>Mauricio Hochschild</b> and the Aramayo family the most powerful men in the country and turning mining camps such as Catavi and Siglo XX into centres of militant unionism.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-bolivia">Republic of Bolivia · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Biblis, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.68,8.45&amp;year=1935" aria-label="Biblis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4744 433 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mauricio Hochschild"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5194 703h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5194 703h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.68,8.45&amp;year=1935">What was here in 1935? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
